{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CXLFvm11rXeLm1s71XMwj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and propose the next task.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as test subjects. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting utilities\n- `magefiles/` - Mage build targets\n\n\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current State**: Specifications are complete for the core packages and utilities like ts, wc, cat, sponge, ls, and du, though the implementations exist at generation-merged tags rather than on the working branch. The rel01.0 release is in progress with focus on ts and testutils, and there's an open issue to implement the differential testing harness for pkg/testutils.\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 - Batch 1 centers on ts specification and differential testing, with the use case in progress but no code started yet.\n\nLooking at the priorities, rel01.0 is the earliest incomplete release with two open issues covering pkg/testutils and cmd/ts. Since I can only take on one task and shouldn't duplicate existing work, I need to assess which issue to tackle next given the constraint.\n\nBoth existing issues already address the core requirements for rel01.0-uc001-ts, so I'm checking what else might be needed beyond these two implementations.\n\nFor rel01.1, the roadmap shows cmd/wc, cmd/cat, and cmd/sponge are coming next, but the constraint limits me to proposing just one task. Since the planning constitution emphasizes focusing on the earliest incomplete release and avoiding duplication, and the open issues already cover what rel01.0 needs, I need to determine whether there's something else blocking rel01.0 or if I should move forward with the next priority. existing issues\"\n\nThe two existing open issues cover rel01.0's needs. So the next task should be for rel01.1 or a dependency needed for rel01.0 that isn't yet covered.\n\nBut wait, looking at prd001-testutils R1-R5, the issue `generation-2026-02-26-23-16-32-dtl` says \"Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001 R1-R5)\". This covers all requirements.\n\nAnd `generation-2026-02-26-23-16-32-imv` says \"Implement cmd/ts timestamp utility (prd004-ts R1-R8)\". This covers all requirements.\n\nSo rel01.0 is fully covered by open issues. The next task should be for rel01.1.\n\nFor rel01.1, I need to pick between cmd/cat, cmd/sponge, and cmd/wc. The planning constitution prioritizes libraries before consumers, but these are all command packages. Going by alphabetical file path order, cmd/cat comes first. All three depend on pkg/testutils which is already being worked on, so that's not a blocker. Since the constitution also requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, cmd/cat would actually be two tasks: the implementation covering R1-R4, and then the tests.\n\nSince I can only propose one task, I'll propose the implementation task for cmd/cat, which covers the core functionality requirements.\n\nLooking at the requirements more carefully, I need to include R5 (error handling and exit codes) as part of the implementation since it's essential to the feature. The task should target the five main requirement groups from prd006-cat, which fits within the 5-8 requirement range for a code task.\n\nFor the Go implementation, I'm estimating around 150-250 lines of production code based on the original cat.c being about 278 lines, which is reasonable for a relatively straightforward utility. I'm mapping each of the five requirement groups (R1 through R5) as a single requirement in the task, accounting for their sub-requirements, and structuring the implementation task accordingly.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific files and requirements: I'll need to create cmd/cat/main.go as the primary implementation, referencing the product requirements spec, architecture documentation, and module configuration. The requirements break down into implementing default file reading behavior with proper stdin/stdout handling, adding line numbering with the -n and -b flags using a specific format, and implementing blank-line squeezing with the -s flag.\n\nBeyond that, I need to handle the display transformation flags (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, -u) with proper caret notation and M- prefix handling, plus robust error handling that exits with the right codes, continues processing after errors, and handles SIGPIPE correctly. The design approach is to apply transformations in the order specified by the requirements and use the module name from go.mod. .9: squeeze blanks (-s), then non-printing display (-v/-T), then end-of-line marker (-E), then line number (-n/-b)\n- D3: Process input byte-by-byte or line-by-line for transformations; use bufio.Scanner for line-oriented processing but switch to byte-level for -v flag as needed\n- D4: -b implies -n (activates line numbering) but skips blank lines per R2.2-R2.3\n\nAcceptance criteria covering output formatting, special character handling, flag combinations, error handling, and code quality checks. I'm counting the requirements to make sure they align with the project scope—looks like I have five core requirements so far. ✓\n- Acceptance criteria: 9 - should be 5-8, so let me trim to 7-8\n- Design decisions: 4 ✓\n\nLet me trim AC:\n- AC1: Compiles without errors\n- AC2: Default mode passes binary data without corruption\n- AC3: `-n` numbers all lines, `-b` numbers only non-blank lines, both with format \"     N\\t\"\n- AC4: `-s` collapses consecutive blank lines to one, applying across file boundaries\n- AC5: `-v` correctly applies caret notation and M- prefix; `-A`, `-e`, `-t` are equivalent combinations\n- AC6: Transformation order matches prd006-cat R4.9: squeeze → non-printing → end-marker → line-number\n- AC7: Exit code 1 on file open error; remaining files still processed; go vet passes\n\nThat's 7 ACs. Now I need to check the planning constitution—implementation and tests should be separate tasks, so this is implementation only. Following the ordering rules, cmd/cat comes before cmd/sponge and cmd/wc for rel01.1. But I should verify that rel01.0 is complete first with the testutils and ts implementations already assigned.\n\nSince rel01.0 is covered, cmd/cat implementation is the right next task to propose.\n\nI'm laying out the full task structure for implementing the cat utility with all its flags and requirements—reading files or stdin, handling line numbering with -n and -b, and managing various output transformations according to the spec. I'm finishing up the display flag specifications—the various combinations like -e and -t are shortcuts for common flag groupings, and all these transformations need to happen in a specific order: squeezing first, then non-printing characters, end markers, and finally line numbers. For error handling, I need to make sure the program exits cleanly on success, handles file open and write errors appropriately by reporting to stderr and continuing with other files, and doesn't treat a closed stdout as a fatal error. The package structure is set up as cmd/cat under the github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils module.\n\nFor the implementation, I'll use bufio.Scanner to process lines, apply the transformations in the fixed order specified, handle byte-by-byte processing for non-printing display, and catch SIGPIPE signals so the program exits gracefully when piped to a closed stdout rather than crashing.\n\nThe acceptance criteria cover compilation, byte-perfect passthrough in default mode, line numbering with -n and -b flags where -b only numbers non-blank lines, and the -s flag collapsing three or more consecutive blank lines into one.\n\nNow I'm specifying the remaining acceptance criteria: -v encodes control characters and high bytes in a specific format while preserving tabs and newlines, -A/-e/-t are combinations of other flags, and error handling for non-existent files should print to stderr, exit with code 1, but continue processing remaining valid files.\n\nI'm reviewing the planning constraints against the PRD—the task correctly implements cat without tests since testing is deferred to a separate task. For partial lines at EOF, the PRD requires cat to preserve them exactly as-is without adding or removing newlines, which means I need to handle byte-level reading carefully rather than relying on line scanners that strip delimiters. I should also verify the SIGPIPE handling requirement in the PRD. I'm realizing that Go handles SIGPIPE automatically in most cases, so I don't need to over-engineer this. Since cat doesn't depend on pkg/sys, I'll keep the SIGPIPE handling straightforward—just catch it with signal.Notify and exit cleanly. Let me finalize the task description now, making sure it stays within the constraint of a single task. Writing the task specification... Looking at the non-printing display flags and their combinations, I need to understand how -v, -E, -T, and -A work together, plus the error handling requirements around file access and stdout failures. The design decisions section is starting to outline the module structure. ukic/go-unix-utils per go.mod; binary entry point is cmd/cat/main.go with package main. I need to apply transformations in the fixed order from prd006-cat R4.9: squeeze blanks first, then non-printing display (-v/-T), then end-of-line marker (-E), then line number prefix (-n/-b). I'll read input line-by-line with bufio.Reader.ReadBytes('\\n') to preserve partial last lines without a trailing newline, avoiding bufio.Scanner which would strip newlines. For SIGPIPE handling, I'll catch syscall.SIGPIPE via os/signal and call os.Exit(0) to match gcat behavior where stdout close by a downstream consumer results in a clean exit. The acceptance criteria cover successful compilation, byte-for-byte passthrough of all 256 values with no flags, proper line numbering formats for -n and -b, and blank line squashing with -s across file boundaries.\n\nNow I'm specifying the detailed behavior for the -v flag and how it interacts with other options like -A, -e, and -t, as well as how the tool should handle non-existent files.\n\nLet me verify the issue structure against the requirements—checking that it stays within the task limit, doesn't duplicate existing work, covers the right acceptance criteria, and has the proper number of design decisions. Everything looks aligned with the PRD, and since cmd/cat doesn't depend on pkg/testutils for its implementation, the dependency marking is correct.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":74253,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":74253},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6e7403f5-9cb3-4a7a-a6d0-7e923b842c5a","uuid":"be98cce8-7f55-4df4-b03c-8a233b904201"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CXLFvm11rXeLm1s71XMwj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development: derive formal specs from C source, generate Go implementations via AI coding agent, verify parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/<utility>/` — Go binaries, one per utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` — Differential testing harness (runs Go + reference binary side-by-side)\n- `pkg/sys/` — Portable syscall abstractions (TIOCGWINSZ, stat metadata, SIGPIPE/SIGWINCH)\n- `pkg/format/` — Shared output formatting (column alignment, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` — Mage build/lint/analyze targets\n- `docs/` — All specs (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current state**: Specs complete for all planned utilities. No generated code on working branch (code lives only at generation-merged tags). Two open issues already cover rel01.0: `dtl` (pkg/testutils) and `imv` (cmd/ts).\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 is in_progress and fully covered by the two existing open issues. The next release is rel01.1 (wc, cat, sponge). By P8 canonical ordering (implementation before tests, alphabetical by primary file path), cmd/cat comes first.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/cat concatenate utility (prd006-cat R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd006-cat.yaml (all flag behaviors, transformation order, exit codes)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ package structure and conventions)\n      - go.mod (module path github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/cat/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: cat utility -- passthrough, line numbering, blank squeezing, non-printing display, error handling\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement default behavior: read named files in order, or stdin when no files are given or '-' is given as a filename; write contents verbatim to stdout; handle binary input (all byte values 0x00-0xFF) without corruption; do not add or remove newlines from the input (prd006-cat R1.1-R1.5)\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement line-numbering flags -n and -b: -n prepends a right-justified 6-wide line number followed by a tab character to every output line using format '%6d\\\\t'; -b prepends a number only to non-blank lines, leaving blank lines without a number or tab; -b overrides -n when both are given; a blank line is defined as a line containing only a newline character (prd006-cat R2.1-R2.4)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement blank-line squeezing -s: collapse two or more consecutive blank output lines to one; the first blank line is written and subsequent consecutive blank lines are discarded; squeezing applies across file boundaries when multiple files are concatenated; apply squeezing before line numbering so suppressed blank lines do not consume line numbers (prd006-cat R3.1-R3.3)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement non-printing display flags -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, -u: -v encodes control characters 0x00-0x1F (except tab 0x09 and newline 0x0A) as ^X where X is 64 positions higher, 0x7F as ^?, and bytes 0x80-0xFF with M- prefix; -E appends '$' before each newline; -T displays tab as '^I'; -A equals -v -E -T combined; -e equals -v -E; -t equals -v -T; -u accepted without effect; apply transformations in order: squeeze blanks, non-printing display, end-of-line marker, line-number prefix (prd006-cat R4.1-R4.9)\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement error handling and exit codes: exit 0 on success; exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened -- print error message to stderr and continue processing remaining file arguments; exit 1 on stdout write error; do not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer (prd006-cat R5.1-R5.4)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Module path is github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils per go.mod; binary entry point is cmd/cat/main.go with package main; no external dependencies beyond the Go standard library\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Apply transformations in the fixed order from prd006-cat R4.9: squeeze blanks (-s) first, then non-printing display (-v/-T), then end-of-line marker (-E), then line-number prefix (-n/-b); this order is non-negotiable per the spec\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Read input line-by-line using bufio.Reader.ReadBytes('\\\\n') rather than bufio.Scanner, so that partial last lines (no trailing newline) are preserved without reconstruction; the newline byte (when present) is included in the returned slice and must be preserved in output\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Handle SIGPIPE by catching syscall.SIGPIPE via os/signal and calling os.Exit(0) so that piping cat output to head or other truncating consumers exits cleanly without a broken-pipe error message, matching gcat behavior (prd006-cat R5.4)\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"'go build ./cmd/cat/' succeeds with no errors and produces a runnable binary\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Default mode: all 256 byte values (0x00-0xFF) pass through without corruption when no flags are active; output bytes are identical to input bytes\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"-n produces format '     N\\\\t' (6-wide right-justified number followed by tab) for every output line including blank lines; -b produces the same format for non-blank lines only, blank lines receive no number and no tab; -b takes precedence over -n when both are given\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"-s with input containing four consecutive blank lines produces exactly one blank line in output; squeezing applies across file boundaries so the last blank line of one file and the first blank line of the next file count as consecutive\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"-v encodes: 0x01 as '^A', 0x1B as '^[', 0x7F as '^?', 0x80 as 'M-^@', 0xA0 as 'M- ', 0xFF as 'M-^?'; tab (0x09) and newline (0x0A) are not altered by -v\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"-A produces output byte-for-byte identical to applying -v, -E, -T simultaneously; -e produces output identical to -v -E; -t produces output identical to -v -T\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"A non-existent file argument causes a descriptive error message on stderr and an exit code of 1; any remaining valid file arguments on the command line are still read and written to stdout before the process exits\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":74253,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":74253},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6e7403f5-9cb3-4a7a-a6d0-7e923b842c5a","uuid":"681de690-2fec-47e6-9670-e5f521d89a27"}
